WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
WESLEY CHUN: Hi, I'm Wesley
Chun from the Google Cloud team,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.350
joined by my colleague,
Martin, to welcome you

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.210
to "Serverless Migration
Station," where

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.750
each video shows developers one
modernization technique for one

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.610
of our serverless
compute platforms.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.250
Today, our little
friend, Porter,

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.440
will take Python 2 App Engine
developers from Cloud NDB

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.000
to Cloud Datastore.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.898
This is an optional
migration, so you

00:00:19.898 --> 00:00:22.440
don't need to make this journey
unless you have a good reason

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.197
to do so.

00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:24.780
MARTIN OMANDER: That
sounds good, Wes.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.410
But wait.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.450
My time is precious.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.160
Why is this migration optional?

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:29.910
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.650
That's a very good
question, Martin.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.140
Well, legacy Python 2
App Engine developers

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.900
have traditionally used
App Engine's NDB library

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.250
to talk to Datastore.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.470
In the previous
module 2 video, we

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.650
showed you how to move from
App Engine NDB and App Engine

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.980
Datastore to Cloud NDB
and Cloud Datastore.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.800
That migration had
one specific goal--

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.630
modernizing App
Engine apps means

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:55.170
moving away from legacy
services to unbundled services,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.600
because the original services
are feature complete.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.640
So all innovation
moving forward only

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.460
comes with the newer,
standalone services.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.220
You can move to Python 3, the
next-generation App Engine

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.790
service, and that's why
this additional migration

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.910
to Cloud Datastore is optional.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.270
By the way, it's
just app migration.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.640
Since App Engine Datastore
and Cloud Datastore

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.650
point to the same
database in your project,

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.170
no data migration is needed.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.240
Review the module
2 video and codelab

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.180
to refamiliarize yourself
with our starting point.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.170
The links are below, as well.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.280
Let's pause for a moment to let
you review before moving on.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.328
Because App Engine Datastore
and Cloud Datastore

00:01:31.328 --> 00:01:33.120
point to the same
database in your project,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.010
one great feature
of that migration

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.710
is that you didn't have to
migrate the data, only doing

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.142
the app migration in the video.

00:01:39.142 --> 00:01:40.350
MARTIN OMANDER: That's right.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.510
And not only did I
watch that video,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.940
I helped you make it, remember?

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.330
Anyway, I know how to
migrate from App Engine

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.360
NDB to Cloud NDB now.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.540
I'm also pretty sure you said,
by migrating to Cloud NDB,

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.030
I'd be accessing
Cloud Datastore.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.910
But didn't you just say we're
migrating to Cloud Datastore

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:00.840
today?

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.060
If I'm already on
Cloud Datastore,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.290
why would you want
me to migrate to it?

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:05.940
I'm confused.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.930
WESLEY CHUN: Oh no, no.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:07.900
It's not that.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.690
Apologies for the confusion.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.650
You're right.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:14.310
In both cases, we're still on
the Cloud Datastore database.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.680
But here, we're
talking libraries.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.650
When Datastore matured to
becoming its own product,

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.860
the Google Cloud team created
a new native client library

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.600
for non-App Engine apps.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.460
That's also where
we point Python 3

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.160
and other next-generation
engine runtimes.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.030
Cloud NDB is made just for
Python 2 App Engine developers.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.120
Today's migration is for those
considering that extra step

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.000
to going from Cloud NDB to
the Cloud Datastore client

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.830
libraries.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.990
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I
think I understand now.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.690
You're saying that there are
two client libraries to access

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.810
Cloud Datastore, one for
long-time Python App Engine

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.130
users who prefer the NDB
style, while the other is

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.470
for those completely
new to Cloud Datastore.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.250
Is that right?

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:56.958
WESLEY CHUN: Yes.

00:02:56.958 --> 00:02:59.130
Essentially, that's
right, although Cloud NDB

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.580
can use with the next-generation
and Python 3 App Engine apps,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.000
as well as non-App Engine
apps, meaning Python developers

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.340
have two options for
accessing Cloud Datastore,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.780
depending on their preference.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.080
Now the real question is why
you would consider migrating.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.910
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that's my point.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.840
If both can be used for App
Engine and non-App Engine apps,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.970
why should I even
consider migrating?

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.090
It'll require some
effort without much gain,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.100
since I'm not getting
any new features

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.332
and generally expect
no changes to my app.

00:03:28.332 --> 00:03:29.040
WESLEY CHUN: Yep.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.570
That is absolutely
correct, which

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.640
is the main reason why
this migration is optional.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.170
If your older App Engine apps
uses Cloud NDB to access Cloud

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.720
Datastore and newer
non-App Engine apps

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.570
use its native client library
and satisfy that everything's

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.040
working, there's really
no need to change.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.490
The only reason you
would consider it

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.090
is if your organization wants to
consolidate to a single client

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.630
library for consistency shared
across all of your apps.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.520
It'll reduce the size
of your code base

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.640
and the one common library would
become robust, because they'll

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.675
be used by many apps.

00:04:00.675 --> 00:04:02.940
It would be fully-featured,
fully debugged,

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.800
and increased code reuse.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.890
This makes code across apps
easier to manage and lower

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.540
maintenance costs overall.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.930
However, if that's not worth
your effort, just stop now.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.029
MARTIN OMANDER:
OK, I'll bite, Wes.

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:19.750
Let's say I want to do that,
or maybe I'll think about it

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.870
and I want to at least see
what this migration entails.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.370
WESLEY CHUN: All
right, sounds good.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.740
OK, everyone, same
drill as before.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.440
Copy your module 2
cloud in the app code.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.270
Or if you don't have
it, clone the repo

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.800
and download the zip file.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.330
The only decision
you have to make

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:36.310
is whether you want to
migrate the Python 2

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.500
version of the app or Python 3.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.750
These are in the module
2a and 2b folders.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.260
You can also migrate the
Python 2 version first,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.580
then port it to Python 3, which
is how the codelab does it.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.330
We'll show you how to
do both in a minute,

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.520
but let's pause now so
you can go grab the code

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.790
and also pull up the codelab so
you can get hands-on experience

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:56.620
with this migration.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.110
Let's go to the computer
and walk you through it.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.170
It's always good to
start with a working app.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.180
From your Python
3 module 2 folder,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.030
run gcloud app deploy
to upload to App Engine.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.740
If you're still in
Python 2, double

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.290
check that Flask
and NDB are in lib.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:14.600
If not or you're unsure,
it's fine to delete

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.480
the lib folder and
reinstall, then deploy.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.490
Your motivation to
upgrade to Python 3?

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.680
No more manual bundling.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.340
Regardless, when deployed, point
a browser or curl to your app

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.020
and confirm the
output is the same

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.500
as from before, whether
modules 0, 1, or 2.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.770
OK, now let's migrate
to Cloud Datastore.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.180
The best part about migrations
is what doesn't change.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.700
app.yaml and
appengine_config.py stay as-is.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.850
Same goes for index.html
and templates.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:47.240
For requirements.txt,
switch from cloud-ndb

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.680
to cloud-datastore.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.313
Also add versions here.

00:05:50.313 --> 00:05:52.730
I left them out of the video,
since version numbers change

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.310
all the time, and also, it's
different between Python 2

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.300
and 3.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:00.410
Regardless, the repo will be
updated, unlike this video.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.620
Python 2 developers must delete
their lib folders and reinstall

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.440
now, while Python 3
users can just chill.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:10.040
When done, we can move on to
the main application file.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.130
At the top of main.py, we're
importing and initializing

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.760
the API client for Cloud
Datastore instead of Cloud NDB.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.040
We also have to add a
new import for datetime,

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.890
because Cloud Datastore doesn't
do automatic timestamps,

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.750
so we have to do it.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.290
For store_visit(), NDB
acts more like an ORM,

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:30.290
or Object Relational Mapper,
where you declare a data model,

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.690
instantiate an object with the
desired data, then save it.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.180
On the other hand,
with Datastore,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.360
you create an entity,
specifying its kind as a key.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:41.060
You get back a dict-like
object to add to your data

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.320
as key-value pairs.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.080
Or in our case, we merge
an entire Python dictionary

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.060
into it, then save.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:50.990
There's also no context manager
when using Cloud Datastore.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.960
In fetch_visits(), you can
see querying is similar,

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.270
but Datastore is a
bit easier to read.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.670
NDB favors chaining
methods together,

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.190
but Datastore has a more
step-by-step approach,

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:04.010
plus the convenience of getting
Python dictionaries back

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.135
without having to do
additional conversion like NDB.

00:07:07.135 --> 00:07:09.260
And that's all that's needed
to migrate this sample

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:10.790
app to Cloud Datastore.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:13.250
Save, gcloud app
deploy, and confirm

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.560
it still works just as before.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.478
Let's go back to the
main presentation.

00:07:18.478 --> 00:07:20.020
MARTIN OMANDER:
Thanks for showing us

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:21.070
that immigration, Wes.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:23.410
It was good to see that
Cloud Datastore isn't

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.778
that different from Cloud NDB.

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.070
WESLEY CHUN: Of course, Martin.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.010
It's true the code is similar,
but this is a sample app.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:32.980
In real life, there are likely
more challenges, especially

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:34.840
related to complex queries.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.460
So I've linked to the queries
pages for both libraries

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.432
so you can see more
differences between them.

00:07:40.432 --> 00:07:42.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, I'm doing
the codelab on my own time.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:47.440
I might need to see the Cloud
NDB and Cloud Datastore docs.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.910
You got those links handy?

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.100
WESLEY CHUN: Yep, I
thought you'd ask.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:53.890
Here are the links to both the
Cloud NDB and Cloud Datastore

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:56.500
docs, as well as to their
client library repos.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.440
Finally, we linked to the
App Engine Datastore docs.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.530
Don't forget the repo has both
the start and finish folders,

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:05.620
so you can reset back
to the 2a or 2b code

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.510
if you take a wrong
turn during the codelab

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:09.550
or want to compare
your answers with ours

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.040
in the module 3a or 3b folders.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.810
By the way, if you're
still wondering

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.540
whether to choose Cloud
NDB or Cloud Datastore,

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.580
here are our recommendations.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.790
Creating a new app in Python
3, App Engine or otherwise?

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:23.710
Go straight to Cloud Datastore.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:26.020
Got a Python 2 NDB
App Engine app?

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.530
Migrate to Cloud NDB,
then port to Python 3.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.900
Lastly, if using Cloud
Datastore elsewhere

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.140
and want a consistent codebase
using only one client library,

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:36.669
consider this migration.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:38.950
If not, don't bother,
because Cloud NDB

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.890
works in both Python 2
and 3 with App Engine

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:43.048
or outside of App Engine.

00:08:43.048 --> 00:08:44.590
So there's really
no need to move off

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.558
unless you have Cloud
Datastore code elsewhere.

00:08:47.558 --> 00:08:48.350
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:49.520
Thanks, Wes.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:51.440
That was a really
good explanation.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.600
It helps me make my decision.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.490
By the way, on
another note, I also

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:58.220
heard that Cloud
Datastore was renamed.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:08:59.730
Is that right?

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:01.250
WESLEY CHUN: Yes, Martin.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:02.520
It's a bit more than that.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:05.090
And I didn't want to distract
users from the main content.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:06.770
But yes, it's true.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.490
The next-generation datastore
service launched in 2017.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.400
As it inherited some features
from the Firebase Realtime

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:17.420
Database, it was rebranded
as Cloud Firestore.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.460
All existing Datastore
databases have

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.210
been upgraded under the covers
to Firestore in Datastore mode.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:26.210
For new applications, consider
using the Cloud Firestore

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:27.375
in native mode.

00:09:27.375 --> 00:09:29.750
We've given you a link to
learn more about when you would

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:31.560
choose one over the other.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.650
Neither the Cloud NDB nor
Cloud Datastore libraries,

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:37.040
however, can access
those Firebase features.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:40.730
You do really need to use the
Cloud Firestore client library.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.520
If you have an app, App Engine
or otherwise, and at some point

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.770
down the road want to take
advantage of those Firebase

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:49.340
features, consider further
migrating to Cloud Firestore.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.540
If interested,
check out module 6.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.640
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that's pretty interesting.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.770
I didn't even know
about Cloud Firestore

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.798
and I will take a
look at that module.

00:09:58.798 --> 00:10:01.090
That sounds like another
"Serverless Migration Station"

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.800
episode we can work on together?

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.870
WESLEY CHUN: That'll
be fun for sure.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.940
What other migrations should
you consider for your App Engine

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:08.440
app?

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.140
If your app uses the original
App Engine task queues,

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.150
consider migrating
to Cloud Tasks

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.880
and check out
modules 7, 8, and 9.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.340
If you're curious about
containerizing your apps,

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.970
check out the App Engine to
Cloud Run, modules 4 and 5.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:25.180
Finally, if your average app
is simple, like our sample app,

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:27.070
consider migrating
to Cloud Functions.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.893
More on that in module 11.

00:10:29.893 --> 00:10:32.310
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds like a
lot of options for our users

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:33.090
out there.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.760
Happy to be working with
you to help guide our users

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.475
and show them best
practices, Wes.

00:10:37.475 --> 00:10:38.850
WESLEY CHUN: Sounds
good, Martin.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:42.600
And that wraps today's optional
migration to Cloud Datastore.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.400
